// WCL feature tour. Subject is the language; wdoc is one feature among many.
page features { sites = [:wcl]
  h1 "Features"

  p "A quick tour through WCL's major features. The [language reference](reference:index) covers each in detail."

  h2 "Typed configuration"

  p "Fields bind names to values; blocks group fields under a kind and a label. Numeric literals carry exact-width suffixes (`u32`, `i64`, `f64`), and strings come in four encodings (`utf8`/`ascii`/`utf16`/`utf32`)."
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
name    = "alpha"
count   = 3u32
enabled = true

service "web" {
  port = 8080u32
  metadata {
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "Schema validation"

  p "A schema declared with decorators describes what's legal at each level. `wcl check` validates a document against its `@document` root, reporting violations with spans."
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0)   name: utf8
  @default(80) port: u32
}

@document
type Config {
  @children("service") services: list<Service>
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "Types, interfaces, and unions"

  p "Records (`type`), structural contracts (`interface`), and tagged sums (`union`) — with record / typeref / unit variants. Use `extends` to inherit fields or variants, and `&T` for an interface-typed reference field."
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
union Shape {
  Circle { radius: f64, stroke: f64 }
  Polygon i32
  Empty
}

interface Drawable { x: f64  y: f64 }
type Scene { focus: &Drawable }
WCL
  }

  h2 "First-class functions"

  p "Functions are values: `fn(params) -> Return body`. They can take and return other functions, and they participate in lazy field evaluation with cycle detection."
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
double = fn(x: i32) -> i32 x * 2
adder  = fn(x: i32) -> fn(i32) -> i32 fn(y: i32) -> i32 x + y
WCL
  }

  h2 "Pattern matching"

  p "`match` tests against literals, bindings, variant patterns, `|` alternatives, `if` guards, the `_` wildcard, and the `..` rest."
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
area = match shape {
  Shape::Circle { radius, .. } => pi() * radius * radius,
  Shape::Polygon(n) if n > 2   => 0.0,
  _                            => 0.0,
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "CLI tooling"

  p "The `wcl` binary parses, checks, evaluates, edits, and reformats source. `wcl eval` resolves a dotted path; `wcl set` rewrites a field in place (following imports); `wcl fmt` reformats canonically; `wcl repl` is an interactive evaluator."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl check site.wcl
wcl eval  site.wcl service.web.port
wcl get   site.wcl name --json
wcl set   site.wcl service.web.port 9090u32
wcl fmt   site.wcl --in-place
wcl repl  site.wcl
BASH
  }
  p "Each subcommand is covered in the [CLI reference](reference:cli)."

  h2 "Language server"

  p "`wcl lsp` is a `tower-lsp`-based language server with diagnostics, formatting, document symbols, go-to-definition (cross-file), find-references, hover, completion, semantic tokens (including inside `${…}` interpolation slots), and quick-fix code actions. It speaks stdio by default and TCP with `--tcp ADDR`."
  callout "Editor stubs" {
    class = ["info"]
    body  = "editors/vscode ships a minimal VS Code extension that spawns wcl lsp for .wcl files, and editors/tree-sitter-wcl is a tree-sitter grammar for editors that consume them."
  }

  h2 "Documentation with wdoc"

  p "`wcl wdoc` renders WCL `page` blocks to a static documentation site — diagrams, charts, syntax-highlighted code, LaTeX math, character-grid terminals, calendar timelines, icon packs, and built-in colour themes. This very site is built with it."
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
site mysite {
  default_template = :webpage
  title            = "My project"
  root             = true
  theme            = :nord
}

page index { sites = [:mysite]  start = true
  h1 "My project"
  p "Authored in WCL, rendered to HTML by **wcl wdoc**."
}
WCL
  }
  callout "Try it" {
    class = ["tip"]
    body  = "wcl wdoc serve docs/main.wcl rebuilds on every .wcl save — see Getting started for the full walkthrough."
  }

  p "Ready to write some WCL? Head to [getting started](getting_started), or jump into the [language reference](reference:index)."
}
